# /libvox-core configuration. Copy to .env and edit.

# --- The two databases ------------------------------------------------------
#
# Both belong to other modules; Core reads them. The Discovery Index is
# /libvox-sync's, the Tenant DB is /libvox-portal's -- and Core is a read-only
# consumer of the second, because that database has exactly one writer.
LIBVOX_DB_DSN=postgresql://libvox_app:libvox_app@127.0.0.1:5435/libvox_index
LIBVOX_TENANT_DB_DSN=postgresql://libvox_app:libvox_app@127.0.0.1:5435/libvox_tenant

# --- Credential decryption --------------------------------------------------
#
# Must be byte-identical to /libvox-portal's: credentials stored under one key
# cannot be read back with another. Decrypted per driver call and never cached.
LIBVOX_CRYPTO_KEY=

# --- The internal API -------------------------------------------------------
#
# /internal/v1 takes tenant_id from the request body, so anything that can reach
# it can read any library's data. Core refuses to start without a token when
# LIBVOX_ENV is anything but `development`.
#
#   export LIBVOX_CORE_SERVICE_TOKEN="$(openssl rand -hex 32)"
LIBVOX_CORE_SERVICE_TOKEN=
LIBVOX_ENV=development

# 8091, because 8080 and 8081 are Koha Testing Docker's OPAC and staff
# interfaces. Loopback by default: publishing this is a decision, not a default.
LIBVOX_CORE_HOST=127.0.0.1
LIBVOX_CORE_PORT=8091

# How long a session credential lives. Short by rule -- opaque, short-lived and
# single-tenant is what bounds a compromise in time as well as blast radius.
LIBVOX_SESSION_TTL_S=3600

# --- The tool layer ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# /libvox-mcp, in streamable-http mode. Not stdio: authorization exists only
# over HTTP, and on stdio every caller is anonymous, so every tool call is
# refused for want of a tenant.
#
#   cd ../libvox-mcp && LIBVOX_MCP_BACKEND=core \
#     LIBVOX_CORE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8091 \
#     LIBVOX_MCP_TRANSPORT=streamable-http python -m libvox_mcp
LIBVOX_MCP_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000/mcp

# --- The model --------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One OpenAI-compatible endpoint. The code defaults to a local Ollama, so a
# developer with a model on this machine configures nothing; the values below
# point at the remote Open WebUI this project benchmarks against -- the same
# endpoint and model as tools/opencode.jsonc, so the agent and the bench
# measure the same thing.
#
# The key is never in this file or any other. Export it in the shell:
#   export LIBVOX_LLM_API_KEY="sk-..."

# Extra fields merged into every request to the model, as a JSON object. Core
# never reads it -- it is the mirror of the opaque data a provider attaches to a
# tool call, which Core also carries back without reading.
#
# It is where a thinking control lives, because every vendor spells it
# differently: Granite takes enable_thinking and reasoning_effort, Gemini takes
# reasoning_effort, Ollama takes think.
#
# Worth measuring rather than assuming: three models of very different sizes all
# answer this catalogue chain 8 of 8, so reasoning before a tool call may be time
# spent reaching the same answer. `./core.sh bench` with and without settles it
# in eight questions.
#
# Malformed JSON is refused at startup rather than ignored.
# LIBVOX_LLM_EXTRA_BODY={"enable_thinking": false}
LIBVOX_LLM_EXTRA_BODY=
LIBVOX_LLM_BASE_URL=http://177.66.206.20:3000/api
# Measured, not chosen: 8/8 on the availability chain with one search per
# answer, at a 4.8s median -- the same answer quality as models three and four
# times its size, and faster than both. Q6_K matters to that claim; the same
# model at three bits would be measuring the quantization instead. `./core.sh
# bench` regenerates the table in the README, and a 9B from a different family
# scored 7/8 with three to eight searches, so this is about the model and not
# about the size.
LIBVOX_LLM_MODEL=hf.co/unsloth/Qwen3.5-9B-GGUF:Q6_K
LIBVOX_LLM_API_KEY=

# A hosted alternative, and the base URL is the part people get wrong. It ends
# at /openai -- the client appends /chat/completions itself -- and it is NOT the
# `:generateContent` endpoint Google's quickstart shows, which Core does not
# speak. Gemini's thinking models need the thought signature carried back, which
# Core already does; see "Pointing Core at Gemini" in README.md.
#
#   LIBVOX_LLM_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai
#   LIBVOX_LLM_MODEL=gemini-flash-latest
# Generous on purpose: a self-hosted open-weight model pays for loading its
# weights on the first request after an idle period, and a timeout shorter than
# the cold start turns a slow first question into an outage.
LIBVOX_LLM_TIMEOUT_S=120

# --- The embedding half of the search index ---------------------------------
#
# A reader asked "tem algo sobre matemática?" against a collection catalogued
# in English and was told the library had nothing. It has the books. No lexical
# index crosses a language -- `matemática` and `mathematics` share no stem in
# any configuration -- so `discovery_biblio` carries a vector beside its
# tsvector, and a search runs both arms and fuses them.
#
# **Commented out on purpose: unset is a supported state, not a broken one.**
# With nothing here the index is built without vectors and every search is
# answered by the lexical arm alone, which is what this did before. Uncomment
# to turn it on, and reimport each collection afterwards -- the column arrives
# empty and only an import fills it.
#
# The gateway and the key default to the chat model's, above, because in every
# deployment so far it is the same service. Both are still overridable.
# LIBVOX_EMBEDDING_MODEL=qwen3-embedding:4b
# LIBVOX_EMBEDDING_BASE_URL=
# LIBVOX_EMBEDDING_API_KEY=

# --- The conversation log ---------------------------------------------------
#
# A database Core owns, on the Tenant DB's PostgreSQL instance. One writer per
# database is the rule; one database per server is not, so this shares an
# instance and writes nothing to libvox_tenant.
#
LIBVOX_CONVERSATION_DB_DSN=postgresql://libvox_app:libvox_app@127.0.0.1:5435/libvox_conversations

# Retention, and only retention. Conversations are kept for ninety days:
#
#   ./core.sh prune --dry-run
#   ./core.sh prune --older-than 30
#
# A SECOND connection string, which is not a redundancy. Forgetting spans every
# library by definition, and the role above cannot: it is subject to row-level
# security, an operator's job sets no tenant scope, and so it sees no
# conversations, deletes none, and commits cleanly. Measured on the dev
# database: 0 of 30 visible. `prune` refuses such a connection rather than
# reporting that it forgot nothing.
#
# The service never loads this. It is read by `prune` and by nothing else,
# which is why a long-lived process does not hold a credential that can read
# across libraries.
LIBVOX_CONVERSATION_ADMIN_DSN=postgresql://libvox:libvox@127.0.0.1:5435/libvox_conversations

# `./core.sh prune` runs in a throwaway container, where 127.0.0.1 is the
# container. Set this to override the host.docker.internal form it uses.
# LIBVOX_CONVERSATION_ADMIN_DSN_DOCKER=postgresql://libvox:libvox@postgres:5432/libvox_conversations

LIBVOX_LOG_LEVEL=INFO
